 "margin_locators": [],
 "observations": [
  "body L1-L4: the heads stand over braces. Ptolemy's numbers in L3 are his apogees at the epoch of Nabonassar, 747 B.C. (♏ 14° 10′, ♍ 2° 9′, ♋ 16° 40′, ♉ 16° 10′, ♎ 1° 10′, given with the epochs of the planets in the Almagest, books IX–XI). For his own time the Almagest has them 8° 50′ greater (8° 51′ for Jupiter): ♏ 23°, ♍ 11°, ♋ 25° 30′, ♉ 25°, ♎ 10°, i. e. 233°, 161°, 115° 30′, 55° and 190°. Against these al-Battānī's apogees are greater by 11° 28′ for Saturn, Mars and Mercury, by 3° 28′ for Jupiter, exactly 8° less, as he says (p. 239, L17-L19), and by 27° 14′ for Venus. The differences of L4 are right; each is 8° 50′ (8° 51′ for Jupiter) more than these.",
  "body L2, L5-L6, L9, L23: the dashes of «al–Battānī» are long dashes (5.3 to 6.1 pt). In L2 the degree sign of «201° 28′» prints as a filled dot.",
  "body L8-L12: 20° 18′ at al-Battānī's 1° in 66 years is 1339.8 years. The 20° 18′ is the 8° 50′ of Ptolemy's reduction to the epoch of Nabonassar and 11° 28′; the 1340 years and the 9° 8′ of L12 come from comparing al-Battānī's apogees with those of 747 B.C. as if they were Ptolemy's for 137 (p. 239, L20-L22). With Ptolemy's apogees for his own time al-Battānī's addition is 11° 28′, 18′ more than the 11° 10′ of his catalogue of the fixed stars (L10). The bar in L12 (9.4 pt) is a minus sign; the sign of equality is 9.7 pt long.",
  "body L13: the second e of «Animadvertendum» and the e after the d of «Iezdegerdis» print like o.",
  "body L13-L15: the year 199 of Yazdegerd begins on 28 April 830, and 1 muḥarram 215 is 27 February 830 in the astronomers' reckoning (28 February in the civil one). The 1 of «(1)» prints without its flag.",
  "body L16-L19: the heads stand over braces; in Ptolemy's row Mars' «106° 40» alone has a degree sign. The differences of L19 are right, but Ptolemy's numbers are again those of the epoch of Nabonassar (L3): against his apogees for his own time the Tabula Probata's are greater by 9° 50′, 11° 32′, 9° 3′, 27° 39′ (Venus) and 11° 0′, close to the 10° 30′ of the 693 years from 137 to 830 at 1° in 66 years.",
  "body L22: a heavy bracket opens Schiaparelli's addition, which goes on to p. 242.",
  "body L25-L28: the heads are set in a smaller type, each over a brace; the dash of «al–Battānī» in the head measures 4.1 pt, a long dash of the smaller type. A thin vertical rule (53.9 pt) divides the table. The longitudes for 140 and 880 follow from Leverrier's elements of note 3 with their annual variations over 1710 and 970 years (236° 40′ 31″, 164° 24′ 34″, 121° 50′ 6″; 251° 8′ 49″, 176° 18′ 47″, 135° 27′ 5″), and the errors are the differences. Ptolemy's column holds the apogees of the epoch of Nabonassar (L3); with his apogees for his own time, 233°, 161° and 115° 30′, his errors are −3° 41′, −3° 25′ and −6° 20′. The bars before the errors (9.6 to 10.4 pt) are minus signs.",
  "body L7, L14, L28: specks after «statuit.», over the 2 of «215» and after the «10» of Mars are not transcribed.",
  "notes_left L2: the hyphen of «234-236» measures 3.1 pt.",
  "notes_left L5-L7: the letters are numbers, set right to left after the figures: ح ب ن 8, 2, 50; ح د ل 8, 4, 30; د د لج 4, 4, 33; ج ج لج 3, 3, 33. The dot of the bā’ and the dots of the two jīms of «ج ج» did not print, so that the jīms look like ḥā’ in its initial form; the letters are read by Nallino's figures. The s of the signs is a small raised italic s; in «4° 33′» the degree sign prints as a filled dot.",
  "notes_right L5-L8: Schiaparelli's addition in brackets; the dash before his name measures 8.6 pt."
 ],
